(7656881, 1246651, 67145, '<td valign="top">when I saw after rollover that you''d merged two images in Photoshop I was confused .. thought that was against the rules unless those two images are petty much the same and you''re doing it to focus stack or hdr or something ..<br/>im really sorry this got a dq  .. it''s a brilliant piece of art .. it  wow''ed me when I saw it after rollover and it wows me now .. and the setup is wonderful  ..<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2020-03-29 23:56:34.</i></td>', 'when I saw after rollover that you''d merged two images in Photoshop I was confused .. thought that was against the rules unless those two images are petty much the same and you''re doing it to focus stack or hdr or something ..im really sorry this got a dq  .. it''s a brilliant piece of art .. it  wow''ed me when I saw it after rollover and it wows me now .. and the setup is wonderful  ..', '2020-03-30 00:53:52.000000', '2020-03-30 00:56:34.000000', 0),
(7656906, 1246651, 16912, '<td valign="top">Thanks for taking the time to comment Roz, at first I thought it was my post editing that was to blame ie using, the word ''merge''. Both images are an identical setting, nothing at all changes other than my lighting and as such I feel it best not to submit any more light painting images as changing the light is pretty much why I do it lol.<br/> <br/>In hindsight I could have just used one of the images then recreated the spotlight in photoshop, that would (I guess) be totally legal but not what I wanted to do.<br/> <br/>Oh and yes, as you say multiple images is for focus stacking, HDR and the ''something'' you mention is light painting lol<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by roz:</b></div><hr/><i> when I saw after rollover that you''d merged two images in Photoshop I was confused .. thought that was against the rules unless those two images are petty much the same and you''re doing it to focus stack or hdr or something ..<br/>im really sorry this got a dq  .. it''s a brilliant piece of art .. it  wow''ed me when I saw it after rollover and it wows me now .. and the setup is wonderful  .. </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Thanks for taking the time to comment Roz, at first I thought it was my post editing that was to blame ie using, the word ''merge''. Both images are an identical setting, nothing at all changes other than my lighting and as such I feel it best not to submit any more light painting images as changing the light is pretty much why I do it lol. In hindsight I could have just used one of the images then recreated the spotlight in photoshop, that would (I guess) be totally legal but not what I wanted to do. Oh and yes, as you say multiple images is for focus stacking, HDR and the ''something'' you mention is light painting lol Originally posted by roz: when I saw after rollover that you''d merged two images in Photoshop I was confused .. thought that was against the rules unless those two images are petty much the same and you''re doing it to focus stack or hdr or something ..im really sorry this got a dq  .. it''s a brilliant piece of art .. it  wow''ed me when I saw it after rollover and it wows me now .. and the setup is wonderful  .. ', '2020-03-30 09:43:51.000000', NULL, 0),
